Dr Webster to move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes that: 

(a) Australia's three major telecommunications providers commenced Temporary Disaster Roaming (TDR) on 1 October 2026 as a measure intended to improve communications during natural disasters affecting regional and remote communities; 

(b) TDR can only operate where an alternative mobile network remains available and has sufficient capacity to accept additional users during a disaster event; 

(c) many regional, rural and remote Australian communities continue to rely on a single mobile network provider, meaning TDR cannot operate when that network fails; 

(d) reliable telecommunications are critical during bushfires, floods and other emergencies, providing access to emergency warnings, evacuation information, emergency services and family communications; and 

(e) forecasts of elevated bushfire risk across many regional areas have reinforced the importance of resilient telecommunications infrastructure ahead of the 2026-27 summer season; and 

(2) calls on the Government to: 

(a) immediately identify those regional, rural and remote communities where TDR is unlikely to operate during a network outage; 

(b) provide a comprehensive strategy to improve telecommunications resilience in disaster-prone regions; 

(c) accelerate investment in mobile coverage, backup power systems and network redundancy across regional Australia; and 

(d) report to Parliament on measures being taken to ensure regional Australians are not left without communications during life-threatening emergency events. 

Mr Leeser to move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes its profound sadness at the passing of Mr Allan Ezzy AM APM, former Mayor of The City of Holroyd on 4 September 2026; 

(2) acknowledges his significant contribution to local government through his 38 years of service as an independent councillor on The City of Holroyd council, including nine terms as Mayor and three terms as Deputy Mayor, during which he was a tireless advocate for the people of Holroyd and Western Sydney; 

(3) recognises his: 

(a) service beyond local government, including his distinguished career in law enforcement and his service to the people of New South Wales as a police prosecutor; and 

(b) commitment to community service through Rotary, church and service organisations, his role in establishing the Greystanes Community Centre, and his mentorship of generations of local councillors and community leaders; and 

(4) extends its deepest condolences to his wife, Patricia, his daughters, grandchildren and great-grandchildren, and records its appreciation for a life dedicated to public service, community leadership and the people of Western Sydney. 

Ms Aldred to move: 

That this House: 

(1) condemns the Government for imposing higher costs on small businesses through the surcharge ban while allowing the Australian Taxation Office to stop accepting credit card payments rather than absorb merchant fees itself; 

(2) notes small businesses are already facing near-record insolvencies, business closures and real profits still below where they were at the change of government, making further cost pressures harder to absorb; 

(3) recognises that local cafés, community pharmacies, tradies and family-run businesses will be forced to carry costs that larger businesses may be better placed to negotiate or absorb; 

(4) calls out the double standard of requiring small businesses to absorb merchant fees while the Government's own tax collector refuses to do the same; and 

(5) calls on the Government to back small business, properly assess the cost of new regulation, and support a Small Business Act that requires Canberra to consider the impact of its decisions before imposing further burdens on small and family businesses. 

Mr Rebello to move: 

That this House: 

(1) condemns the Government for failing to get inflation under control; 

(2) notes Australians have now faced a 16th interest rate rise, placing further pressure on families, mortgage holders and small businesses; 

(3) recognises the rising cost of everyday essentials under the Government including: 

(a) housing up 23.9 per cent; 

(b) electricity up 37.0 per cent; and 

(c) insurance up 43.3 per cent; 

(4) calls out the Government's high spending, with spending at its highest share of the economy since 1986-87 outside the pandemic; and 

(5) calls on the Government to rein in spending, reduce inflationary pressure and ease the cost-of-living burden on Australian families and small businesses. 

Mr Small to move: 

That this House: 

(1) agrees that Australians deserve a migration system in which they can have confidence, and of which migrants can be proud to be part, one that strengthens our economy, fills critical skills gaps, eases pressure on housing and essential services, and helps build a better Australia; 

(2) condemns the Government for allowing unprecedented levels of migration to place unsustainable pressure on housing, infrastructure, roads, schools and hospitals, and making it harder for Australians to buy a home; 

(3) notes the Opposition's plan to reduce net overseas migration to 100,000 a year for the first two years and reduce the number of temporary visa holders by around 650,000 over four years; 

(4) acknowledges the Opposition's commitment to raise migration standards by prioritising skilled migrants who meet Australia's genuine workforce needs; 

(5) recognises the Opposition's plan to strengthen the integrity of the migration system by ensuring temporary visas are genuinely temporary and reducing opportunities for visa hopping. 

Mr Tehan to present a Bill for an Act to amend legislation relating to climate change, electricity and nuclear energy, and for related purposes. (Scrap Net Zero (Cheaper Energy) Bill 2026) 

Ms Sharkie to move: 

That this House: 

(1) recognises that: 

(a) Australians continue to face significant cost of living pressures, with high fuel prices increasing expenses for households, business, transport operators and community groups; 

(b) people living in regional, rural and remote communities are disproportionately affected by high fuel prices because of limited or non-existent public transport options and long distances to travel to access work, education, health care and essential services; and 

(c) diesel prices remain particularly high, increasing freight and transport costs that flow through to the price of goods and services, placing additional pressure on family budgets and regional economies; 

(2) acknowledges that: 

(a) the current fuel excise on petrol and diesel is 53.7 cents per litre; 

(b) national average fuel prices remain elevated, with diesel prices substantially higher than unleaded petrol prices; 

(c) higher fuel prices increase goods and services tax (GST) revenue collected by the Commonwealth, as GST is applied to the final retail price of fuel, including the fuel excise component; 

(d) previous temporary reductions in fuel excise have demonstrated that the Commonwealth has the capacity to provide immediate relief to motorists and businesses during periods of exceptional fuel price increases; and 

(e) while fuel tax credits provide relief for eligible off-road fuel use, many regional households, small businesses, transport operators and community organisations receive no equivalent support for their on-road fuel costs; and 

(3) calls on the Government to: 

(a) reduce the fuel excise on petrol and diesel by 50 per cent for a minimum period of three months; 

(b) ensure the full benefit of the excise reduction is passed on to consumers; 

(c) monitor the impacts of the reduction on households, small business and regional communities; and 

(d) continue pursuing long-term measures to improve Australia's fuel security and resilience. 

Ms Sharkie to move: 

That this House: 

(1) recognises that: 

(a) Australia's agricultural sector relies on a strong and reliable seasonal workforce, with Working Holiday Makers filling around one in seven farm jobs and playing a critical role in regional economies and food production; 

(b) Australian university and TAFE students represent a significant potential seasonal workforce during the summer holiday period, while gaining employment, experience and greater exposure to regional Australia; and 

(c) a strong seasonal workforce is essential to support farmers, regional communities and Australia's food and fibre supply chains; 

(2) acknowledges that: 

(a) recent changes to the Working Holiday Maker Program, including a ballot system and reduced visa numbers, have prompted concerns from agricultural industries about the availability of seasonable labour; 

(b) the National Farmers Federation and other industry groups have warned that workforce shortages and visa processing delays could affect agricultural productivity, food security and regional economies; and 

(c) additional workforce pathways are needed to complement existing agricultural labour programs; and 

(3) calls on the Government to: 

(a) investigate the establishment of a national primary production seasonal work incentive program for Australian university and TAFE students that provides up to $500 HECS-HELP debt credits per week (in addition to award wages) for approved regional seasonal work; 

(b) work with universities, TAFEs, agricultural organisations and employers to connect students with seasonable employment opportunities in regional Australia; 

(c) ensure participation in seasonal work does not unfairly disadvantage students receiving income-tested government support; and 

(d) continue consultation with the agricultural sector to ensure workforce policies support the labour needs of regional Australia. 

Mr Katter to move: 

That this House: 

(1) condemns the continued decline of essential postal and banking services in regional, rural and remote Australia, threatening the viability of country post offices and the communities they serve; 

(2) recognises that a post office in the regions is not merely a business but an essential community lifeline for pensioners, farmers, small businesses and entire communities; 

(3) notes that when a post office closes, residents are left travelling for hours to access basic services, including the elderly, people with disabilities, and those without reliable internet, who bear the greatest burden; 

(4) rejects a system that sacrifices essential community services in pursuit of narrow commercial returns, treating regional Australia as an afterthought rather than an integral part of the nation; and 

(5) calls on the Government to take immediate and decisive action to secure postal operations in regional and remote Australia by: 

(a) demanding Australia Post has a leadership and approach that enable a future for regional Australia, committed to preserving and strengthening essential services in rural, regional and remote communities; 

(b) intervening to ensure no regional or remote community is abandoned without reasonable access to essential postal, banking, and face-to-face financial services; 

(c) delivering permanent, properly funded arrangements to keep vulnerable licensed post offices operating, including a proposed minimum annual support payment of up to $100,000 for eligible regional, rural and remote outlets; 

(d) supporting the expansion of Australia Post's role in delivering essential banking services, including consideration of a publicly backed postal banking model to restore access to financial services in communities abandoned by the major banks; and 

(e) further demanding that Australia Post returns to its fundamental obligation to serve all Australians regardless of postcode or commercial profitability and protect the vital role of country post offices in sustaining regional communities. 

