Raise Our Voice in Parliament 

Mr DAVID SMITH (Bean—Government Whip) (16:19): This speech comes from Matilda from Bean: 

My name is Matilda, I am 13 years of age, and I am proud to have two parents that both work in the trades. I have had the opportunity to watch my parents build a bright future for me and my little sister. Despite that, our family has received discrimination because my parents wear hi-vis for a living. 

There is still a held belief that a person from a blue-collar industry is somehow un-educated or is of less value than someone that attended university. It is often drilled into young people that university is the only option to choose for a good career path. 

I know this is not true. My father lays bricks, working tirelessly to construct the buildings that we use every day, like this great house my speech is being heard in. He has helped build schools, rec-centres, hospitals, and homes for families. My mother works as a high voltage electrician, in an industry that is building green infrastructure; like big batteries, wind farms, and solar energy, all of which will help make this planet a healthier place. 

Both my parents have jobs that can be done from anywhere, allowing us the freedom to explore and live in different places. Neither have student debt, and both earned a wage while they got their education, enabling them to move into their industries debt free. 

I believe that greater information needs to be given at younger ages that university is not always the best option for a bright future. This needs to be encouraged in school aged children so that we meet the skills shortage that we face in my generation. 

Lili, also from Bean, highlights the importance of mental health: 

My name is Lili. In Australia today, 1 in 7 children aged 4-17 experience a mental health disorder every year. If that's happening in our classrooms, how can we act on it? 

I know how this feels because I've felt and seen this before, the terrible feeling of feeling lonely and helpless. 

Shouldn't every school in Australia have equal access to mental health support. Right now, only half of schools have regular access to a psychologist. The government must expand programs like Be You. If this program was fully funded and strengthened, every school could receive proper mental health training, resources, and support. 

Government should also fund wellbeing kits with mindfulness cards, stress relief tools, and wellbeing journals. I would definitely use them! 

I believe that all of you have seen a child suffering quietly in the dark, perhaps that child was you, recall that exact feeling, that feeling is what we are trying to prevent. 

Thank you, Lili and Matilda, for both raising your voices. Our government is listening and acting and agrees that these are priorities that all governments should be concerned with. 

La Trobe Electorate: Roads 

Mr WOOD (La Trobe) (16:22): The Dandenong Ranges is a beautiful place to live, but in summer we always have the fear of fires. Many roads right across the Dandenong Ranges are unsealed. Under the former, Liberal government we actually committed $150 million to Cardinia council to deliver 110 kilometres of sealed roads across 164 local roads. This was an initiative put forward to me by CRAG21, a local group very much pressing that need. Why would people in the hills get second-rate roads compared to, say, a greenfield or new estate, where they get brand new roads with kerbs and channels? When you're living up in the hills—and I've lived up there pretty much all my life—and you live on an unsealed road, you have, obviously, more potholes than you could possibly imagine, which is hard when you're living in Victoria now. There are more potholes there, and there's dust. But the big concern is bushfires, because, on many of these roads, the kerb or the drainage could be nearly a metre deep. So, if there's a fire and people are driving through smoke, it can be exceptionally dangerous. I want to thank Paul Keen and his team at the Cockatoo Road Action Group for the 21st century, CRAG21, for tirelessly advocating on behalf of the hills. 

The reason I'm mentioning this today is that, sadly, when the Albanese government came to power, they withdrew $109 million from the project, leaving just $41 million from the original commitment. It went to the Suburban Rail Loop. So it was diverted from unsealed roads in the hills to those ripping off and rorting the Big Build, as we call it—or the big rort, it should be called, when it came to the CFMEU. Now we've had even the Victorian premier, Ben Carroll, acknowledge that the money must be better spent and they're trying to find ways of saving money. First of all, to the Albanese government: you need to reinstate that funding. It's so bad that so many local residents were planning on having their roads upgraded and working with Cardinia council, and the money was taken away. Where did it go? As I say, to the Suburban Rail Loop—the biggest rort around. The money needs to be returned, and I call on the Albanese Labor government to return that money. Finally, I'll just say it was a matched election commitment when Bill Shorten was the leader, and, sadly, Labor broke their promise and reneged on it. 

Raise Our Voice in Parliament 

Ms STANLEY (Werriwa—Government Whip) (16:25): Again this year, young people in my electorate have submitted speeches to the Raise Our Voice program. This is Tanja's contribution: 

Next year I turn eighteen, and I will walk into a polling booth for the first time. 

Most Australians will vote without ever being taught how our democracy works. 

I've sat in classrooms where an election came and went without a single lesson on it. 

Australia is rightfully proud of our 90% voting turnout. But turnout isn't understanding, and that gap doesn't sit there quietly. 

We've seen it get used against us. 

Not long ago, the Voice referendum got reduced to a single slogan: "if you don't know, vote no." That wasn't clever messaging. 

It was aimed straight at a gap we built ourselves. That gap won't close on its own. 

I am asking Parliament to make civic education a real, compulsory part of every school, properly funded, not a one-off excursion, with our understanding shaped before approaching the ballot, not after. 

Because a vote without understanding isn't democracy. It's a ritual. 

Democracy was never something we simply inherited at eighteen. 

It's something we have to be taught to understand, trusted to question, and trusted to shape. Every young person in Australia deserves civic education as a right, not a hope. 

Anything less is a failure of our democracy. 

Nour's contribution is about housing. Nour is 15: 

For my generation, housing is more than the cost of buying a home; it shapes almost every decision we make about our future. 

Growing up, I was taught that hard work, contribution and responsibility could lead to a secure future. 

Today, that promise feels increasingly out of reach. Young Australians are delaying moving out, starting families, pursuing careers and choosing where to study because affordable housing is becoming harder to find. 

Housing is not just an economic issue; it is a question of fairness and opportunity. When essential workers cannot afford to live near the communities they serve, those communities become weaker. When young people spend years worrying about rent instead of building their futures, Australia loses potential. 

Parliament has the opportunity to act by increasing affordable housing supply and ensuring new homes are supported by infrastructure like transport, schools, healthcare and jobs. We must build connected, liveable communities where people can belong. 

My generation is not asking for an easy path to home ownership. We are asking for a fair chance to work hard, contribute and build a stable future. 

The decisions made today will determine whether the Australian dream remains within reach for future generations. 

Thank you to Nour and Tanja for your contributions to this year's Raise Our Voice. 

Monash Electorate: Roads 

Prostate Cancer 

Ms ALDRED (Monash—Opposition Whip) (16:28): I'd like to associate myself with the remarks made by the member for Werriwa on the need to strengthen and support civics education in our schools. 

In just 10 days time, we will have the MotoGP on Phillip Island for the final time because, to the great disappointment of our state, the Victorian state Labor government have lost the MotoGP to South Australia. But in 10 days time we want to put on a really good show for everyone travelling to wonderful Phillip Island—and yet our local roads are in disrepair. We will be hosting motorbike riders from all over the state and, indeed, all over Australia. There are many motorcycle clubs that come from South Australia and other places for our great event. That is why the Liberal candidate for Bass, Rochelle Halstead, and I are jointly calling on the Victorian state government to send emergency road crews down, right now, to fix those roads. They are unsafe. Unfortunately, we have had a number of accidents. Tragically, we've had a motorcyclist die recently because of a pothole in Victoria. That is not good enough. We deserve better. Rochelle Halstead and I are calling on the state Labor government to get its act together and fix the roads. 

It's a privilege to rise on behalf of a very important issue to my local community on behalf of constituents like Jason, a tradie who owns his own business in Drouin; like Craig from Lardner Park and their great team, who in fact have their next Baw Baw Big Blokes Barbecue to raise funds for prostate cancer in a couple of days time; and like the Hon. Jim Lloyd, a former minister in this place, who've shared their concerns with me about the funding withdrawal for prostate cancer nurses from next year. The coalition shares the concern of the Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia and the broader prostate cancer community in what appears to be a rushed and ill considered decision to cut funding from this vital program. I want to commend the big blokes barbecue volunteers who have their event on in Lardner Park shortly. This group has raised $2.2 million in 12 years for prostate cancer support and men's health while supporting prostate cancer awareness to the more-than-700 blokes in attendance. It is a great program. It's a great initiative. Across Australia, more than 130 Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia specialist nurses work across 100 sites, delivering more than 150,000 consultations every year. This support is absolutely vital to the men that they support and their families. I want to commend that work to this House and call on a reversal of the funding outcome for this great initiative. 

Gellibrand Electorate: Roads 

Mr WATTS (Gellibrand) (16:31): Residents of Point Cook in my electorate have been stuck in traffic for too long, so I congratulate the state member for Point Cook, Mathew Hilakari, and the Carroll Labor government for their announcement that a re-elected state Labor government will commit $195 million to duplicate Point Cook Road between Central Avenue and Dunnings Road. Point Cook Road connects Australia's biggest suburb to the rest of Melbourne, and, from the freeway exit to Point Cook Homestead Road, it stretches about five kilometres, linking people with friends, family, work and leisure. For most of that length, it's one lane each way, and that's because it was built for a different Point Cook. At the turn of the century, around 2,000 people lived in Point Cook in what was a rural hamlet at the fringe of the city. Today, around 70,000 people call it home. We're forecast to reach 85,000 in the coming decade. Nearly 28,000 vehicles use Point Cook Road every day in the peak, with locals commuting to work via the Princes Freeway and parents doing the school drop-off and pick-up. Ask any of them, and they'll tell you that the road is no longer fit for purpose. 

The Albanese Labor government knows that growing suburbs in Melbourne's west need infrastructure investment that grows with them. This commitment doesn't stand alone in that respect. It complements the federal government's $3.5 million in funding for the business case to duplicate Point Cook Road between Dunnings Road and Jamieson Way and the $55 million in federal funding to duplicate and upgrade Central Avenue between Lunn Court and Skehan Boulevard, feeding into the Central Avenue and Point Cook Road intersection. 

Fixing the Central Avenue intersection is necessary for fixing Point Cook Road, and these works are helping traffic move through the intersection, easing congestion at the top of Point Cook Road and providing improved interchanges with the Princes Freeway. Put those together, as well as the Victorian government's commitment, and you get a pipeline of works that moves section by section down Point Cook Road from Central Avenue, with each project building on the last. That's what you get with state and federal Labor governments working together. You can already see the flow of traffic improve as works are completed. At Central Avenue, the old roundabout is gone, and the new traffic lights are now operating. Further down Point Cook Road at the Sneydes Road intersection, traffic lights have also been installed at an intersection that so many locals once tried to avoid. 

We know roadworks can be disruptive, but each phase is an investment in getting our community to where they're going faster and safer. I thank the workforce, who have delivered this project faster than expected. I thank the community, who have put up with a period of time of cones and detours along the way, and I know that Point Cook has waited a long time for this road to be fixed, but we're getting it done, because fixing this road doesn't just mean less time spent in traffic. It means less money spent on fuel and more money in people's back pockets. Most importantly, it gives locals time back in their day, easier school drop-offs, better access to good jobs and more time with their family. We'll keep working with the state Labor government to deliver what Melbourne's west needs. 

Hinkler Electorate: Disaster and Emergency Management 

Hinkler Electorate: Emergency Services 

Mr BATT (Hinkler—Deputy Nationals Whip) (16:34): As the weather warms up, communities across Hinkler are preparing for another summer of storms, flooding and possible cyclones. Our fire season is already proving active and volatile, and that is why I'm pleased Labor was forced late last week to reverse its senseless move to slash disaster recovery funding to Queensland. This was a bad decision from the outset. Once again, Labor has been forced to back down. I stood alongside the Queensland LNP government, including our local state MPs, Stephen Bennett; David Lee; and John Barounis to demand this decision be overturned. I also advocated alongside the Local Government Association of Queensland and our mayors, Helen Blackburn and George Seymour. Together we made the voices of Hinkler heard, and common sense prevailed. 

Back in June, the federal government announced plans to split disaster recovery funding 50/50 with states and territories, having previously contributed up to 75 per cent, but Labor failed to properly consult or explain the decision to those carrying the load on the ground. When disaster strikes, Aussies expect Canberra to step up, not shift the bill to the states and call it reform. Last week, Hinkler participated in Queensland's annual awareness program, Get Ready Queensland. I had the fortune of visiting Bundaberg's aerial firefighting hub, which supports our region and all of Queensland. 

The airport hosts an impressive range of world-class firefighting aircraft, including Black Hawk helicopters and the large aerial tanker. With a retardant capacity of 10,000 litres, the tanker helps slow fires, strengthen containment lines and support crews working in difficult terrain. The LAT, as it's known, is a welcome, regular visitor to our region, supporting the Queensland Fire Department's fleet of 14 contracted aircraft. I thank Peter Hollier, the Deputy Chief Officer of Central Region Rural Fire Service Queensland, for assisting with the visit and everyone involved in keeping Queensland safe this fire season. 

On Saturday night, I had the privilege of attending Bundaberg Central Rotary Club's 13th annual Brian Prince Awards, recognising outstanding service in our emergency services. These awards honour the late Brian Prince, a former rotarian and paramedic who always put service above self. It was special to have Brian's wife, Laurel, and son, David, there. The ambulance service award went to Officer Gary Stockwell and the Police Service Award went to Senior Constable Thomas Bell. Senior Firefighter Chris Sheather received the Queensland Fire Department Award, while Jenny Hinds and Aaron Quay were both recognised for their service with Wide Bay Hospital and Health Service. The LifeFlight award went to Captain Tony Miller. Queensland Ambulance Service volunteer Brian Rose was named Volunteer of the Year. 

To all of Hinkler's emergency service personnel and volunteers, those who run towards danger and keep us safe, thank you. 

Newcastle Knights 

Koori Knockout 

Ms SWANSON (Paterson) (16:37): Famously, former Newcastle Knights captain Matty Johns said that the 1997 grand final win was 'better than LEGO'. Just a couple of weeks ago, the Hunter was buzzing again at the thought of the Knights chasing another Provan-Summons trophy. We could see it everywhere. We could feel it—red and blue jerseys, flags flying and footy being talked about at the shops, at work, at the pub and at the club. Everyone was getting behind our boys and hoping this would be another year for the mighty Knights. People were painting their houses red and blue. Within days, the Hunter was transformed into a red-and-blue wave of excitement and energy. 

So many Novocastrians and people from right across the Hunter wanted to be part of the moment that after tickets sold out in minutes special venues had to be opened to ensure that fans could get together to watch the game. Even my beloved Heddon Greta Drive In opened up so that local families could get together and share the excitement on the big screen at the drive-in—but don't slash the seats if you don't like the outcome, kids! I can assure you a Roosters jersey was a rare sight in the Hunter that week, except for maybe on Pat Conroy, my lovely colleague. On grand final night, plenty of us gathered around the television to cheer them on. 

Unfortunately, it wasn't to be. To lose a grand final by one point is just heartbreaking, but we'll come back again. Those boys did us proud. To go from last year's wooden spoon to playing in the biggest game of the year was an extraordinary achievement. The Knights gave our region so much to cheer about, and they should be incredibly proud of what they've accomplished. 

While we were all caught up in the excitement of the grand final, there was another huge celebration of rugby league happening right in our backyard as well. Over the October long weekend, the Koori Knockout was played in Raymond Terrace—where my office is, on Worimi country—bringing around 40,000 people to our region. And what a fantastic event to host! The Koori Knockout is about so much more than football; it's about bringing people together to celebrate sport, culture and family and just have a great time. Our very own Newcastle Yowies gave us plenty to cheer about, taking out the women's first-grade title for a second year running with a 14-6 win over the Redfern All Blacks. Congratulations to the Yowies. What a wonderful achievement! 

But I just want to say: you know what? We're working on our footy. We'll get out the Lego. We'll build again. We'll be back next year for another cracking season of football. 

Shepparton Art Museum 

Horse Racing 

Mr BIRRELL (Nicholls) (16:40): I rise to speak about the Shepparton Art Museum, or SAM, and, in particular, its successful hosting of works from Australia's favourite and most significant art award, the Archibald Prize. The exhibition coincided with a visit to Greater Shepparton by Her Excellency the Governor-General, Sam Mostyn. It was a happy coincidence, because the portrait by artist James Powditch titled Once Upon a Time in Yarralumla was part of the exhibition—a portrait of the Governor-General. We found ourselves admiring the portrait, and, as the Governor-General said later, it was Sam looking at Sam with Sam at SAM! There was a time when SAM couldn't host such exhibitions; it only had room to display a very small fraction of its own collection. But that changed when the coalition government backed a new museum on the Victoria Park Lake in Shepparton with $15 million. Since opening, the new museum has hosted numerous exhibitions and added significantly to the cultural heart of Shepparton. Along with the Archibald, the Young Archie Shepparton presents 26 portraits created by local students aged between five and 18 years of age, and those portraits are of people near and dear to them. 

Over the coming weeks, there will be a lot of focus on racetracks around the country—particularly in our home state of Victoria, Madam Deputy Speaker Aldred—with the Spring Racing Carnival, culminating on 3 November with the running of the Melbourne Cup at Flemington. And the racing industry extends way beyond the track. Northwood Park stud near Seymour, one of the many breeding operations, is in my electorate. Just down the road, you'll find a statue of Black Caviar, the champion racehorse who won all of her 25 starts. She was bred at the nearby Gilgai Farm near Nagambie, in my electorate. The Australian thoroughbred-breeding industry is the second-largest in the world. It supports nearly 10,000 jobs and contributes around $1.7 billion to the national economy. Northwood Park stud is part of the international Godolphin organisation, and it's an incredibly modern and sophisticated set-up on the Goulburn River—again, in my electorate of Nicholls. The farm has a long history as a horse and cattle property originally developed by AG Hunter. He raced and stood the 1925 Melbourne Cup winner, Windbag, on the property. That racehorse, Windbag, has a few namesakes in this place from time to time! Everyone, enjoy the racing and remember that there are thousands of hardworking staff—vets, farriers, feed suppliers and horse transporters—behind every runner during the Spring Carnival. 

Disaster and Emergency Management 

Carers 

Ms KARA COOK (Bonner) (16:43): Today marks the International Day for Disaster Risk Reduction, a reminder that the best time to prepare for a disaster is, of course, long before it strikes. In South-East Queensland, that message couldn't be any more timely. As we enter the high-risk weather season, Brisbane is facing high temperatures, severe thunderstorms, heavy rainfall and hail this week. In light of the rain we've had there today, I'm waiting at the moment to hear from my local netball club about whether my daughter will be playing tonight. In Bonner, we know the devastating impacts of extreme weather. From the 2022 floods to ex-tropical-cyclone Alfred last year—as a community, we loaded thousands of sandbags into cars at the Lota depot over the weeks that led up to that disaster—we've seen roads and homes inundated, power losses for days and local businesses left picking up the pieces. That's why the Albanese Labor government is taking practical steps to strengthen Australia's disaster preparedness. 

Through our $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund, we're investing in programs that help communities withstand future disasters. We're delivering AusAlert, a new national emergency warning system designed to get critical information to Australians faster. Of course, we've also confirmed last week that the Commonwealth will continue to deliver up to 75 per cent of eligible disaster recovery funding, with no changes to the existing arrangements, ahead of this higher risk weather season. But disaster resilience starts at home, too. I encourage everyone across Bonner to access the preparedness resources through the National Emergency Management Agency, of course also monitor the Bureau of Meteorology warnings and have an emergency plan in place, because we can't control the weather, but we can make sure that our communities are ready when it turns. 

