These amendments legislate a humanitarian exemption across the terrorist organisation offences in sections 126 to 128 and the terrorism-financing offences in sections 131 and 132 of the Criminal Code. The exemption would be both subject based, covering qualifying humanitarian organisations and their financial and implementing partners, and activity based, covering the funds, resources and services needed solely to deliver humanitarian assistance in time. This is 100 per cent consistent with and is as a result of the UN Security Council resolution 2664 in 2022, which I understand the Australian government has supported. 

Why do we need this? I particularly want to thank ACFID and many of those humanitarian organisations who have been asking for this exemption for a significant amount of time. Aid workers in places like Gaza and Afghanistan have to deal with whoever controls the ground in order to reach people who are starving, who are dying, who need life-saving care. That can mean paying a checkpoint fee or a local tax or a utility bill to an authority linked to a listed organisation. There is no other way to save people's lives. There's no other way to provide the food, to provide the medicine, to provide the shelter. Under our current laws, that can expose an Australian aid organisation and its staff to the risk of terrorism charges, carrying extremely long prison terms—simply for the act of giving someone food when they're starving. 

The Criminal Code already recognises this problem in a narrow way, with a defence to the association offence for providing humanitarian aid. But there's no equivalent protection for the funding and support offences, which is exactly where aid groups face the most risk. They've told us, they've told the coalition and they've told the Labor Party. They've said it, and they've asked for this help. Not doing this will have a chilling effect: organisations pulling back, banks refusing transactions, help not arriving and people dying. That's the effect of not doing this: people dying. The UN Security Council recognised this in 2022 when it adopted resolution 2664, a standing humanitarian carve out for UN sanction regimes. In September 2025 this government signed the Declaration for the Protection of Humanitarian Personnel—and we welcome that—and committed at a ministerial level to implementing humanitarian exemptions to sanctions and counterterrorism measures. But where are they? 

This amendment just delivers on that government promise. And there's a real consistency here. This bill expands criminal protections for UN and humanitarian personnel, and the government says it reflects Australia's leadership on humanitarian protection. That part of the bill we welcome. But you cannot claim to be protecting aid workers with one part of the Criminal Code while leaving them exposed to prosecution under the other part. For those reasons, I commend these amendments to the Senate.

