Mr FARLEY (Farrer) (13:11): I move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes that: 

(a) Australia is the driest inhabited continent on Earth; 

(b) the Millennium Drought (1997-2009) and the Tinderbox Drought (2017-2019) exposed the vulnerability of the nation's water supplies, regional communities, food production and natural environment, and the lessons of those droughts are yet to be acted upon; 

(c) the Intergenerational Report 2026: Australia's future to 2066 projects that Australia's population will grow from 26.5 million in 2022-23 to 40.5 million by 2062-63, an increase of more than 50 per cent; 

(d) emerging industries, including artificial intelligence data centres, are creating new and substantial demand for water that was not contemplated when the Water Act 2007, the Murray-Darling Basin Agreement, and the Murray-Darling Basin Plan were developed; 

(e) projected growth in demand for drinking water, irrigated food and fibre production, and industrial and technology uses will exceed the capacity of the nation's existing water storage and supply infrastructure; and 

(f) Australia's record in conceiving, planning, approving and delivering major water infrastructure has been marked by protracted delay, and the nation does not have the luxury of time; 

(2) acknowledges that: 

(a) the Murray-Darling Basin Plan is not delivering for Basin communities, the Basin environment or the nation; 

(b) national water security cannot be achieved by reallocating water within the Murray-Darling Basin alone; and 

(c) future planning must consider water resources outside the Basin to complement and augment supply; 

(3) recognises that water security underpins both the economy and the environment, and that these are mutually inclusive to Australia's future prosperity; and 

(4) calls on the Government to establish a national water accord that: 

(a) provides an overarching national framework above the Water Act 2007, the Murray-Darling Basin Plan and the Murray-Darling Basin Agreement; 

(b) determines Australia's future water demand, future sources of supply, and the full capital cost of the infrastructure required to store and deliver that supply; 

(c) is whole-of-government, drawing in the portfolios of treasury, finance, infrastructure, agriculture, and regional development, and secures the cooperation of the states and territories; 

(d) draws on expertise in hydrology, civil engineering, environmental science and infrastructure finance, and on the knowledge of the communities affected by water policy; 

(e) commits to investment in new storages, modernised irrigation networks and water efficiency projects to deliver, as a matter of priority, an additional 2,000 gigalitres of water, financed through 50-year intergenerational funding models; and 

(f) ensures that the water infrastructure required for a population of 40 million or more is installed and operational well before 2050. 

Australia is approaching a defining moment in its national history. We're planning for millions more Australians—more housing, more industry, more energy and more food—yet this parliament has failed to answer one fundamental question: where is the national water strategy that is capable of sustaining the Australia we are promising to build? For 20 years, governments have argued over how to divide the water we already possess. The next 50 years demand something far greater. We must secure the water, the infrastructure and the productive capacity future generations will depend upon. Australia needs a national water accord, not another isolated environmental view and not another bureaucratic plan. Australia needs a national commitment bringing every level of government together to secure Australia's economic, environmental and sovereign future. 

The Water Act of 2007 was introduced under the Howard government; the Murray-Darling Basin Plan was introduced under Labor in 2012. Both major parties administered the framework; both must accept responsibility for its consequences. Billions of taxpayer dollars have been committed to water recovery, yet where is the comprehensive accounting of environmental, economic and social returns? Where is the assessment of productive capacity lost, investment foregone, employment displaced and regional economies weakened? These are questions of national accountability. Environmental success must be measured by ecological outcomes, not simply by water recovered or money spent. Economic consequences must be measured beyond the farm gate. 

In Farrer, water policy is an agricultural producer deciding whether to risk planting another crop; a winery or a rice mill deciding whether it can retain its workforce; and a young family deciding whether their town has a future. Water supports machinery suppliers, transport operators, processors, contractors, retailers and entire communities. When productive water is withdrawn, the consequences extend throughout the regional economies. Factories lose throughput, investment retreats and employment contracts. Communities lose the economic purpose that sustains them. Water does not simply grow crops; water builds economies. Australia desperately needs productive economies. 

Our nation carries more than $1 trillion in gross Commonwealth debt. Interest payments consume an increasing share of national revenue. Every dollar servicing debt is unavailable for hospitals, defence, infrastructure and essential services. We cannot borrow our way indefinitely into prosperity; we must produce our way towards economic strength. That means productive industry, stronger exports, more employment and a broader taxation base. Productive irrigation must be recognised within that national economic equation. Every major water decision should face a fundamental test: what does it contribute to GDP, employment, taxation revenue and sovereign productive capacity? Environmental outcomes matter, but so does our capacity to finance Australia's future. We cannot responsibly plan for millions more citizens while treating water security as an afterthought. More people require more food, housing, electricity and reliable water. New industries, including data centres, will place additional demands upon our resources. One certainty hangs over every national projection: Australia will experience another prolonged drought, and the question is whether this parliament has prepared the nation to withstand it. 

Food security cannot be invented during a crisis. Neither can dams, irrigation systems, processing facilities or skilled workforces. These capabilities take decades to develop and, once dismantled, they cannot simply be switched back on. National security begins with the capacity to feed and sustain our people. The Murray-Darling Basin governs a river system. Australia's future demands a national framework. A national water accord must unite Commonwealth, state and local governments, and producers, industry and regional communities. It must establish measurable commitments to water security, food production, environmental outcomes and new infrastructure. It must secure profitable industries, generating employment and exporting taxation revenue to service national debt. and it must place water at the centre of treasury, infrastructure, population, industry, and national security policies. 

Water cannot remain an environmental discussion. Water does not need another 20 years of arguing. It needs the courage to build tomorrow's security. A national water accord is simply— (Time expired) 

